<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Mongnai State</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1959 CE (approx.) · capital Mongnai</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the most senior of the southern Shan states in Myanmar, whose ruler led Shan resistance to the British in the 1880s.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Mongnai, Shan Mong Nai, was one of the older Shan principalities of the southern plateau, with a ruling house traced in Shan tradition to the early 14th century. It was regarded as among the leading states of the southern Shan region.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its saopha ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> valleys and hill country populated by Shan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pao-people">Pa-O</a> and other peoples, paying tribute to Burmese kings in Ava and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>, and sending daughters to the royal court. Rivalries with neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kengtung-state">Kengtung</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/yawnghwe-state">Yawnghwe</a> often turned violent, and Konbaung kings intervened in its successions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 1880s the Mongnai saopha joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limbin-confederacy">Limbin Confederacy</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thibaw-min">King Thibaw</a> and then the British, but submitted in 1887. Mongnai became part of the British protected Shan states and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federated-shan-states">Federated Shan States</a>, and its ruler gave up his powers in 1959.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mongnai-state.jpg" alt="Mongnai State" width="320" height="189"><figcaption>Image: Great Britain India Office · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shan-S-IGI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1959 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mongnai</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Myanmar</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mongnai, Mone, Moene</dd></dl></aside></div>

